WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.900
This is a dam made of
packed dirt, twenty-four

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.719
metres tall, standing on sand and gravel.

00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:14.063
Every dam like this leaks,
through it and under it.

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:18.360
The water comes out near its foot,
which engineers call the toe.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.543
Clear water at the toe is just a leak.

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:25.423
Cloudy water means the dam
itself is coming out with it.

00:00:25.773 --> 00:00:29.033
In one big study of earth
dam failures, nearly half

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:32.135
went this way: water carried
the soil out of them.

00:00:32.485 --> 00:00:36.386
So how does a trickle at the
toe bring down a whole dam?

00:00:38.736 --> 00:00:40.872
First, why soil stands at all.

00:00:41.222 --> 00:00:44.891
Its grains press on each other,
and friction needs that press.

00:00:45.241 --> 00:00:48.041
Water in the gaps between
the grains pushes them

00:00:48.041 --> 00:00:50.582
apart, and takes part of the press away.

00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:52.464
You've seen it at the beach.

00:00:52.814 --> 00:00:56.074
As the tide goes out, there's
a glassy strip of sand near

00:00:56.074 --> 00:00:59.455
the water, where water held
in the beach seeps back out.

00:00:59.805 --> 00:01:02.591
Stand there and wiggle
your feet, and they sink.

00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:06.657
Under a dam, the water coming
up is pushed far harder.

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:10.827
To measure how hard, engineers
use head: the height water

00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:13.833
would climb to in a thin
tube pushed into the ground.

00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:17.062
At the lake, the head
is about twenty metres.

00:01:17.412 --> 00:01:19.688
At the toe, it's down to ground level.

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:23.242
All of that head is lost
on the way under the dam.

00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:26.286
So how hard can that lost head push?

00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:32.226
Say we fill a tube with a metre of this
sand, and feed it water from below.

00:01:32.576 --> 00:01:36.570
The tank starts level with the top
of the sand, and nothing moves.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.821
The grains at the bottom press on each other
with the weight of the sand above them.

00:01:41.171 --> 00:01:42.611
Now raise the tank.

00:01:42.961 --> 00:01:47.883
Water climbs through the sand, and as it
squeezes through the gaps, it loses head.

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.856
That lost head is a push
on the grains, upward.

00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:55.266
The higher the tank,
the harder the push, and

00:01:55.266 --> 00:01:57.453
the less the grains press on each other.

00:01:57.803 --> 00:02:02.029
Raise the tank about a metre above
the sand, and the press is gone.

00:02:02.379 --> 00:02:03.726
The bed boils.

00:02:04.076 --> 00:02:05.330
Here's why it's a metre.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.606
Soaked sand weighs about
twice as much as water.

00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:12.956
Under water, the water around it
holds up half of that weight, so

00:02:12.956 --> 00:02:16.525
it presses down only about as
hard as the same amount of water.

00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:19.995
So if the water loses a
metre of head for every metre

00:02:19.995 --> 00:02:23.145
it climbs, it carries
the sand's whole weight.

00:02:23.495 --> 00:02:26.835
Head lost per metre is
called the gradient, and the

00:02:26.835 --> 00:02:29.811
one that floats the sand
is the critical gradient.

00:02:30.161 --> 00:02:32.436
For most sands, it's about one.

00:02:32.786 --> 00:02:36.966
That's the beach strip, with the water
pushed hard enough to lift the sand.

00:02:37.316 --> 00:02:40.976
And it's the idea behind this
whole series, at its limit:

00:02:40.976 --> 00:02:43.771
the grains stop pressing
on each other at all.

00:02:46.121 --> 00:02:47.328
Now our dam.

00:02:47.678 --> 00:02:50.438
This is the same dam as
in the last episode,

00:02:50.438 --> 00:02:52.787
now standing on the
course's sand and gravel.

00:02:53.137 --> 00:02:56.713
The course's seepage solver
works out where the water goes.

00:02:57.063 --> 00:02:59.803
These lines are the paths the water takes.

00:03:00.153 --> 00:03:03.493
Nine tenths of the leak dives
into the sand and gravel,

00:03:03.493 --> 00:03:06.236
runs under the dam, and
comes up beyond the toe.

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:10.446
On average, under the dam,
the water loses only a fifth

00:03:10.446 --> 00:03:13.274
of a metre of head for
each metre it travels.

00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:17.107
But look where the paths crowd
together, as they turn up at the toe.

00:03:17.457 --> 00:03:20.243
There, the push is four times the average.

00:03:20.593 --> 00:03:22.404
That's about zero point eight.

00:03:22.754 --> 00:03:25.448
That's under one, so
the sand isn't boiling.

00:03:25.798 --> 00:03:28.956
Engineers call soil washing
out of a dam piping.

00:03:29.306 --> 00:03:34.275
The margin against it is one divided by
that push: here, about one point three.

00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:36.389
Designers want at least three.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:38.829
For a new dam, they want four.

00:03:39.179 --> 00:03:43.870
The course warns that its number right at
the toe is a guide, not a measurement.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.263
Either way, this margin is thin.

00:03:47.513 --> 00:03:51.507
So say a boil starts anyway,
at a weak spot near the toe.

00:03:51.857 --> 00:03:57.012
The water carries sand away, grain by
grain, and a hole opens under the dam.

00:03:57.362 --> 00:04:02.563
The dam above holds up its roof, so the
hole stays open, and grows into a channel.

00:04:02.913 --> 00:04:04.585
And the channel is a shortcut.

00:04:04.935 --> 00:04:08.475
Water in it flows freely,
so all the head is now

00:04:08.475 --> 00:04:11.436
lost in the shorter stretch
of sand ahead of it.

00:04:11.786 --> 00:04:15.206
Here's a teaching model on the
same solver: an open channel

00:04:15.206 --> 00:04:18.613
along the base of the dam,
growing ten metres at a time.

00:04:18.963 --> 00:04:20.821
Watch the push at its tip.

00:04:21.171 --> 00:04:23.075
It climbs with every step.

00:04:23.425 --> 00:04:26.125
By the time the channel is
halfway under, the push

00:04:26.125 --> 00:04:29.090
is past one, and the
leak has nearly doubled.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.134
Near the lake end, the push reaches three.

00:04:32.484 --> 00:04:35.177
And the leak is five times what it was.

00:04:35.527 --> 00:04:37.942
A shorter path means a steeper push.

00:04:38.292 --> 00:04:41.171
A steeper push means faster erosion.

00:04:41.521 --> 00:04:45.004
And faster erosion means a longer
channel, and a shorter path.

00:04:45.354 --> 00:04:50.556
The pipe feeds itself, until the roof
falls in and the lake pours through.

00:04:50.906 --> 00:04:55.410
In some sands, a pipe keeps growing
at a far gentler push than one.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.126
So inspectors walk the toe looking
for boils, cloudy water and sinkholes.

00:05:01.376 --> 00:05:03.094
This isn't just a model.

00:05:03.444 --> 00:05:06.404
In June nineteen seventy-six, Teton Dam in

00:05:06.404 --> 00:05:08.738
Idaho was being filled for the first time.

00:05:09.088 --> 00:05:12.848
It was an earth dam ninety-three
metres tall, with a core of

00:05:12.848 --> 00:05:16.751
windblown silt, built against
heavily cracked volcanic rock.

00:05:17.101 --> 00:05:20.581
Both official investigations found
that water carried the silt of

00:05:20.581 --> 00:05:24.253
the core into open cracks in the
rock, with nothing in the way.

00:05:24.603 --> 00:05:27.017
It wasn't a boil at the toe.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:31.487
But it was the same trouble: soil
that washes out easily, water

00:05:31.487 --> 00:05:34.426
moving through it, and an
open way out for the grains.

00:05:34.776 --> 00:05:39.513
It took about five hours from the first
seepage seen near the dam to the breach.

00:05:39.863 --> 00:05:41.163
Eleven people died.

00:05:41.513 --> 00:05:45.093
Across earth dam failures, water
carrying soil out has brought

00:05:45.093 --> 00:05:47.783
down more dams than floods
pouring over the top.

00:05:49.033 --> 00:05:51.959
So the obvious fix is to stop the water.

00:05:52.309 --> 00:05:57.556
Back to our dam: try a wall in the ground
under it, halfway down through the sand.

00:05:57.906 --> 00:06:00.275
The water just goes under the wall.

00:06:00.625 --> 00:06:05.687
The leak falls by less than a tenth,
and the margin stays far short of three.

00:06:06.037 --> 00:06:08.697
Take the wall all the
way down to rock and it

00:06:08.697 --> 00:06:11.517
works, where rock is
close enough to reach.

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:14.346
Now try the opposite.

00:06:14.696 --> 00:06:18.276
Build the toe of the dam from
a filter: clean, carefully

00:06:18.276 --> 00:06:20.872
graded sand, right where
the water comes out.

00:06:21.222 --> 00:06:24.101
More water comes out
now, about a tenth more.

00:06:24.451 --> 00:06:29.095
But where water still leaves the ground
unfiltered, the push drops to under a tenth.

00:06:29.445 --> 00:06:32.139
The margin against piping
jumps to thirteen.

00:06:32.489 --> 00:06:34.254
A filter doesn't stop water.

00:06:34.604 --> 00:06:35.672
It stops soil.

00:06:38.522 --> 00:06:42.144
In the last episode, sand
like this dried out a slope.

00:06:42.494 --> 00:06:46.488
Here, it does its other job:
it keeps the grains in place.

00:06:47.738 --> 00:06:51.081
But how can anything let
water through, and not sand?

00:06:51.431 --> 00:06:53.521
Look at three grains, touching.

00:06:53.871 --> 00:06:56.797
The gap between them is
about a sixth of their size.

00:06:57.147 --> 00:07:01.367
In a real filter the grains come
in many sizes, and its openings

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:04.252
end up about a fifth the
size of its small grains.

00:07:04.602 --> 00:07:07.542
Say the sand at the toe
is like beach sand, most

00:07:07.542 --> 00:07:09.478
of its grains about a
third of a millimetre.

00:07:09.828 --> 00:07:14.054
The engineer Karl Terzaghi gave
two rules for the filter it needs.

00:07:14.404 --> 00:07:17.284
First, the filter's small
grains can be at most

00:07:17.284 --> 00:07:19.931
four times the size of
the sand's big grains.

00:07:20.281 --> 00:07:23.578
Then its openings are smaller
than the sand's big grains.

00:07:23.928 --> 00:07:26.488
So those big grains jam
at the filter's face,

00:07:26.488 --> 00:07:28.990
and hold back the small ones behind them.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.900
Second, the filter's small
grains must be at least

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.866
four times the size of
the sand's small grains.

00:07:35.216 --> 00:07:38.876
Then water passes through the
filter at least sixteen times as

00:07:38.876 --> 00:07:42.368
easily as through the sand, so
no push builds up inside it.

00:07:42.718 --> 00:07:46.858
Terzaghi's very first filter
was this episode's fix: sand

00:07:46.858 --> 00:07:50.427
with weight on top, at the
toe of a dam built on sand.

00:07:51.677 --> 00:07:53.860
It works inside real dams too.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.930
In June nineteen ninety-six,
crews checked a small pothole

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.711
on the crest of the Bennett
Dam, in British Columbia.

00:08:01.061 --> 00:08:03.987
The dam is a hundred and
eighty-three metres tall.

00:08:04.337 --> 00:08:08.749
Under the pothole, they found a column of
loosened core, about eighty metres deep.

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:12.219
It ran down a pipe for a
survey marker, put in while

00:08:12.219 --> 00:08:14.811
the dam was built, and
left off the drawings.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:19.573
An expert panel later found that seepage
had washed part of that core away.

00:08:19.923 --> 00:08:23.503
The fine grains that washed out
got into the filter layer beyond

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:26.796
the core, and filled its
gaps until it held the rest.

00:08:27.146 --> 00:08:30.861
Since then, the panel wrote, it
has worked as a filter should.

00:08:31.211 --> 00:08:34.787
The holes were grouted, and
the dam is still in service.

00:08:36.037 --> 00:08:37.987
Every dam leaks.

00:08:38.337 --> 00:08:41.657
After Teton, the independent panel
wrote that the design should have

00:08:41.657 --> 00:08:45.118
provided measures to render the
inevitable leakage harmless.

00:08:45.468 --> 00:08:47.325
That's what a filter does.

00:08:47.675 --> 00:08:51.875
It's the series' idea once more:
soil is strong when its grains press

00:08:51.875 --> 00:08:55.245
on each other, and water pushing
up through it can take that away.

00:08:55.595 --> 00:08:59.032
A filter lets the water out,
and keeps the grains pressing.

00:09:00.282 --> 00:09:02.836
Every dam so far holds back water.

00:09:03.186 --> 00:09:06.646
Some hold back mine waste,
built from the waste itself, and

00:09:06.646 --> 00:09:09.687
raised for decades on the
soft, wet sand behind them.

00:09:10.037 --> 00:09:13.195
Chile and Brazil both banned
one way of building them.

00:09:13.545 --> 00:09:14.520
Why that one?
